# lattice

your knowledge, on your ship.

Write pages in markdown, gemtext, or HTML, or write Hoon pages that compute their own content. Keep a private, tagged memory you share with your AI. Search all of it in full text. Publishing is peer-to-peer: every published page is addressed as urb://~ship/path and travels ship-to-ship over remote scry. No DNS, no web server, no host in the middle.

(You may well be reading this page through lattice right now.)

## One store, every surface

* The ship. A lattice nexus in the grubbery framework. Pages and knowledge live as grubs in the vault. Published pages answer remote scry, and followed files push you updates.
* The web. A reader, a workspace editor with live preview and drag-and-drop upload, a knowledge browser, and an installable PWA, all served straight from the ship. lattice hosts its own client. There is nothing to install.
* The filesystem. lattice-fs mounts your pages as local files. grep runs from RAM, and you edit in your own editor.
* The agents. MCP knowledge tools compiled into the ship, served at /grubbery/mcp. Your assistant's memory lives on your ship.

## What pages look like

Publish a page, and anyone on the network can read it instantly, straight from your ship:

```
# ~nisfeb's notebook

Welcome. Everything here is a page on my ship.

=> urb://~nisfeb/notes/urbit  Notes on Urbit
=> urb://~nisfeb/now          What I'm doing now
```

A page can also be Hoon that computes its content, with state, commands, and dependencies on other pages. A page or a whole folder can go clearweb-public, served over plain HTTP from your ship.

## What it isn't

* Not a host. Bring your own ship: yours, a friend's, or a hosted one.
* Not the HTTP web. Published pages are Urbit-native and move between ships over remote scry.
* Not an app to install. The client is served by your ship. The only local piece is the optional filesystem client.

## Privacy

The only server lattice talks to is your ship. No analytics, no third-party SDKs, no telemetry. Published pages are public by design. The knowledge store is private by default, and foreign access is deny-by-default at the ship.

